{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ViGtu1SKnYrtMZPinpmohP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n12\t\\usepackage[colorlinks=true,citecolor=blue,linkcolor=blue]{hyperref}\n13\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n14\t\\renewcommand{\\bibsection}{\\section*{References}}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcommand{\\fone}{F\\textsubscript{1}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\fstagone}{F\\textsubscript{1,stag}}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{Stagnation Signals Are Structural: An Empirical Study of\\\\Progress Detection in LLM Agents via Weak Supervision}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{Anonymous}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\\maketitle\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\tLLM-based agents frequently enter stagnation during long-horizon tasks---executing plausible but collectively ineffective action sequences.\n28\tWe present a systematic empirical study of progress stagnation detection through weak supervision over agent trajectories.\n29\tOur central finding is that stagnation signals are fundamentally structural: 30 lightweight features (command repetition ratio, file diff frequency, action entropy) achieve 0.995 in-domain and 0.967 cross-agent macro-\\fone{}, decisively outperforming neural embedding probes (0.906/0.751) and an LLM-as-judge baseline by over 23 percentage points.\n30\tHuman annotation of 650 trajectory windows confirms this hierarchy is not a circular evaluation artifact: on gold labels, structural features attain 0.91 \\fone{} vs.\\ 0.85 for the best probe and 0.68 for LLM-as-judge, with an 8.5pp circularity gap that inflates all learned methods equally.\n31\tWe further discover that labeling functions exhibit systematic temporal dynamics---command repetition leads stagnation onset by 1.87 steps while outcome backpropagation lags by +6.7 steps---but these roles are agent-specific, with command repetition reversing from leading to lagging on a different agent framework.\n32\tDespite these well-characterized temporal patterns motivating a temporal label model, three implementations (time-weighted voting, HMM, lag-aware HMM) all fail to surpass simple majority vote, with time-weighted labels degrading downstream classifiers by up to 21.7pp.\n33\tOur results redirect agent monitoring from expensive representation learning toward lightweight structural approaches.\n34\t\\end{abstract}\n35\t\n36\t%======================================================================\n37\t\\section{Introduction}\n38\t\\label{sec:intro}\n39\t%======================================================================\n40\t\n41\tLLM-based coding agents such as SWE-Agent \\cite{yang2024sweagent} and OpenHands \\cite{wang2024openhands} can autonomously solve software engineering tasks, yet they frequently enter prolonged periods of \\emph{stagnation}: the agent executes commands that appear diverse and locally reasonable but collectively fail to make progress toward the goal.\n42\tDetecting such stagnation in real time is essential for efficient resource allocation, early intervention, and runtime monitoring of autonomous agents.\n43\t\n44\tPrior work has approached agent failure detection through cycle detectors that identify literal action repetition \\cite{jiang2024cdcs}, process reward models (PRMs) that score individual steps \\cite{xi2026agentprm}, and LLM self-reflection mechanisms \\cite{shinn2023reflexion}.\n45\tThese methods either miss semantically diverse stagnation (cycle detectors), require expensive outcome labels and RL pipelines (PRMs), or rely on the judgment of the very model that is stuck (self-reflection).\n46\tRecent evidence from SlopCodeBench \\cite{slopcodebench2026} shows that agent degradation---including verbosity inflation in 90\\% of trajectories---is pervasive, motivating systematic monitoring approaches.\n47\t\n48\tWe study stagnation detection as a window-level binary classification problem: given a window of $k{=}5$ consecutive agent actions, predict whether the agent is making progress or stagnating.\n49\tWe construct labels via a weak supervision pipeline with three labeling functions (LFs)---command repetition, file diff absence, and outcome backpropagation---aggregated by majority vote.\n50\tUsing this framework, we conduct a systematic comparison of structural features, neural embedding probes, and LLM-as-judge baselines across two agent frameworks (SWE-Agent and OpenHands) on 12{,}000+ trajectory windows derived from SWE-bench Verified instances.\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n53\t\\begin{enumerate}\n54\t\\item \\textbf{Structural signal discovery.} We show that 30 lightweight structural features achieve near-perfect in-domain stagnation detection (macro-\\fone{} 0.995) and strong cross-agent transfer (0.967), outperforming the best neural probe by 6--9pp on human-annotated gold labels and LLM-as-judge by 23pp. This is not a circular artifact: human annotation of 650 windows quantifies an 8.5pp circularity gap while confirming the structural$\\,{>}\\,$probe$\\,{>}\\,$LLM hierarchy.\n55\t\n56\t\\item \\textbf{LF temporal dynamics analysis.} We characterize systematic temporal roles of LFs---command repetition leads stagnation onset by $\\sim$1.87 steps on SWE-Agent but reverses to lagging on OpenHands---providing empirical guidance for weak supervision on sequential data and quantifying circular evaluation.\n57\t\n58\t\\item \\textbf{Temporal label model: a well-motivated negative result.} Three temporal label model variants fail to improve over majority vote despite well-characterized i.i.d.\\ violations, revealing that in the low-conflict regime of the current LF framework, temporal alignment introduces more noise than signal.\n59\t\\end{enumerate}\n60\t\n61\t\n62\t%======================================================================\n63\t\\section{Related Work}\n64\t\\label{sec:related}\n65\t%======================================================================\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{Agent Failure Detection.}\n68\tCycle detection methods identify literal action repetition in agent trajectories \\cite{jiang2024cdcs, specra2025, trajad2025}.\n69\tWhile effective for repetitive loops, they miss semantically diverse stagnation where commands differ but collectively fail to advance.\n70\tSlopCodeBench \\cite{slopcodebench2026} recently benchmarked coding agent degradation, finding verbosity inflation in 90\\% of trajectories and output erosion in 80\\%.\n71\t\n72\t\\paragraph{Process Reward Models.}\n73\tAgentPRM \\cite{xi2026agentprm} trains step-level reward models via TD/GAE for evaluating ``promise and progress.''\n74\tOther PRM work includes general frameworks \\cite{prm2025framework}, tool-use benchmarks \\cite{toolprmbench2026}, and trajectory-level approaches \\cite{trajreward2026}.\n75\tPRMs require outcome labels and RL pipelines---they are training signals, not monitoring tools.\n76\tOur approach requires no outcome labels and operates at the window level for real-time monitoring.\n77\t\n78\t\\paragraph{Agent Self-Reflection.}\n79\tReflexion \\cite{shinn2023reflexion}, LATS \\cite{zhou2023lats}, and Tree of Thoughts \\cite{yao2023tree} enable agents to evaluate and correct their own reasoning.\n80\tThese are intervention mechanisms that rely on the stuck model's own judgment, whereas we study external monitoring.\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{Weak Supervision and Data Programming.}\n83\tSnorkel \\cite{ratner2017snorkel} introduced programmatic labeling via labeling functions aggregated by a generative label model.\n84\tExtensions address sequential data \\cite{safranchik2020weakly} and temporal dependencies \\cite{shin2022universalizing}.\n85\tOur work applies weak supervision to agent trajectories and empirically tests whether temporal label models improve over majority vote in this domain.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Agent Monitoring.}\n88\tRecent work argues for sequence-level monitoring of LLM agents \\cite{agentmonitor2025iclr}, and weak-to-strong monitoring has been studied for safety \\cite{weakstrong2025}.\n89\tWe focus on progress monitoring using structural signals.\n90\t\n91\t\n92\t%======================================================================\n93\t\\section{Method}\n94\t\\label{sec:method}\n95\t%======================================================================\n96\t\n97\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n98\t\n99\tGiven an agent trajectory $\\tau = (a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_T)$ of actions, we partition it into overlapping windows $w_t = (a_t, \\ldots, a_{t+k-1})$ of size $k{=}5$.\n100\tThe task is binary classification: $y_t \\in \\{\\textsc{progress}, \\textsc{stagnation}\\}$."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01H4Vmi7xGEJaYwTtAZcN8aE","session_id":"7aba9a09-ed96-4e10-95c4-11fb49122c38","uuid":"ca00b00b-a696-4723-9c7f-7ddcd0abd2bf","timestamp":"2026-05-14T22:23:49.083Z"}
