 of trajectories---is pervasive, motivating systematic monitoring approaches.\n47\t\n48\tWe study stagnation detection as a window-level binary classification problem: given a window of $k{=}5$ consecutive agent actions, predict whether the agent is making progress or stagnating.\n49\tWe construct labels via a weak supervision pipeline with three labeling functions (LFs)---command repetition, file diff absence, and outcome backpropagation---aggregated by majority vote.\n50\tUsing this framework, we conduct a systematic comparison of structural features, neural embedding probes, and LLM-as-judge baselines across two agent frameworks (SWE-Agent and OpenHands) on 12{,}000+ trajectory windows derived from SWE-bench Verified instances."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JPpAymd7RoVNiBpnEnu2yg","session_id":"7aba9a09-ed96-4e10-95c4-11fb49122c38","uuid":"7fab236f-6967-4c83-8743-6d28eeb5652c","timestamp":"2026-05-14T22:23:54.665Z"}
