{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VYP6PBHDwAYPXQxjvi7DAE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n11\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{Structural Features Provide Strong Baselines for Agent Progress Stagnation Detection:\\\\A Gold-Validated Empirical Study with Weak Supervision}\n15\t\n16\t\\author{Anonymous}\n17\t\\date{}\n18\t\n19\t\\begin{document}\n20\t\\maketitle\n21\t\n22\t% ============================================================\n23\t\\begin{abstract}\n24\tWe present a systematic empirical study of progress stagnation detection in LLM coding agents through weak supervision.\n25\tThree labeling functions (LFs)---command repetition, file diff frequency, and outcome backpropagation---generate noisy window-level labels that are aggregated via majority vote.\n26\tOur central finding, validated on a 650-window human-annotated gold set, is that stagnation signals are fundamentally structural: 30 handcrafted features achieve 0.84 gold macro-F1 in-domain and an estimated 0.82 cross-agent, surpassing neural embedding probes (0.79--0.81) and an LLM-as-judge baseline (0.71).\n27\tHowever, circular evaluation---where models trained on LF-derived labels are evaluated against those same labels---inflates reported performance by up to 15.5 percentage points for structural methods.\n28\tWe introduce a hybrid ensemble (structural + probe) that achieves the best gold-validated performance (0.86 macro-F1) by leveraging complementary semantic signals in regions where LF coverage is weakest.\n29\tWe further characterize LF temporal dynamics: command repetition leads stagnation onset by ${\\sim}1.87$ steps on SWE-Agent but reverses to a lagging indicator on OpenHands, where its removal improves F1 by 21.6pp.\n30\tDespite these well-motivated temporal patterns, three temporal label model variants all fail to improve over majority vote---a negative finding that constrains future research on sequential weak supervision.\n31\tOur results support lightweight structural monitoring as the primary detector, with semantic probes providing targeted complementary value, and highlight circular evaluation as a methodological pitfall for the weak supervision community.\n32\t\\end{abstract}\n33\t\n34\t% ============================================================\n35\t\\section{Introduction}\n36\t\\label{sec:intro}\n37\t\n38\tLLM-based coding agents such as SWE-Agent \\citep{yang2024swe} and OpenHands \\citep{wang2024openhands} can autonomously resolve software engineering tasks, yet they frequently enter periods of \\emph{progress stagnation}---sequences of actions that appear locally reasonable but collectively fail to advance toward the goal.\n39\tUnlike simple repetition loops detectable by cycle checkers, semantic stagnation involves diverse commands and file modifications that mask the lack of genuine progress.\n40\tRecent benchmarking confirms this is pervasive: SlopCodeBench \\citep{slopcodebench2026} reports verbosity increases in 90\\% of long trajectories and quality erosion in 80\\%.\n41\t\n42\tExisting approaches to agent progress assessment fall into several categories, none fully addressing the monitoring use case.\n43\tProcess Reward Models (PRMs) \\citep{agentprm2026,prm2025,toolprmbench2026} provide step-level training signals but require outcome labels and RL pipelines.\n44\tSelf-reflection methods \\citep{shinn2023reflexion,yao2024tree} rely on the stuck agent's own judgment.\n45\tCycle detectors \\citep{SpecRA,TrajAD} catch structural repetition but miss semantically diverse stagnation.\n46\tSequence-level monitoring has been advocated for safety \\citep{iclr2025monitoring,weaktostrong2025} but not systematically studied for progress detection.\n47\t\n48\tWe frame stagnation detection as a window-level binary classification problem and study it through weak supervision.\n49\tThree labeling functions (LFs) generate noisy labels from observable signals; majority vote aggregates them.\n50\tWe then compare four detection paradigms: (1)~structural feature classifiers, (2)~neural embedding probes, (3)~LLM-as-judge, and (4)~a hybrid ensemble.\n51\t\n52\tOur study makes five contributions:\n53\t\\begin{enumerate}\n54\t    \\item \\textbf{Structural baseline discovery.} Structural features provide a strong, efficient, and transferable baseline for stagnation detection, achieving 0.84 gold-validated macro-F1 in-domain and 0.82 estimated cross-agent---decisively surpassing probes (0.79) and LLM judge (0.71).\n55\t    \\item \\textbf{Hybrid complementarity.} Semantic probes contribute complementary signals in LF-weak regions (conflict windows, cmd\\_rep-only stagnation, high command diversity stagnation), yielding a hybrid ensemble at 0.86 gold macro-F1.\n56\t    \\item \\textbf{Circular evaluation analysis.} We quantify a ``circularity gradient'' where methods closer to LF inputs suffer greater evaluation inflation (structural: 15.5pp, probe: 11.5pp, LLM judge: $-$4.8pp), offering methodological guidance for weak supervision evaluation.\n57\t    \\item \\textbf{LF temporal dynamics.} We characterize systematic, agent-specific temporal roles of LFs and demonstrate that command repetition's role reversal across agents has actionable implications for LF design.\n58\t    \\item \\textbf{Temporal label model negative finding.} Three temporal label model implementations (TWV, HMM, lag-aware HMM) all fail to improve over majority vote, with TWV degrading downstream classifiers by up to 21.7pp.\n59\t\\end{enumerate}\n60\t\n61\t% ============================================================\n62\t\\section{Problem Formulation}\n63\t\\label{sec:method}\n64\t\n65\t\\subsection{Window-Level Stagnation Detection}\n66\t\n67\tGiven an agent trajectory $\\tau = (a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_T)$ of actions, we partition $\\tau$ into overlapping windows of size $k$ (default $k{=}5$).\n68\tEach window $w_t = (a_{t}, \\ldots, a_{t+k-1})$ is labeled as \\textsc{Progress} or \\textsc{Stagnation}.\n69\t\n70\t\\subsection{Labeling Functions}\n71\t\n72\tThree LFs vote independently on each window:\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{Command Repetition (cmd\\_rep).} Flags stagnation when the ratio of repeated commands within a window exceeds a threshold. Fires at 97.4\\% of windows on SWE-Agent, making it the dominant signal. It leads stagnation onset by ${\\sim}1.87$ steps on SWE-Agent but reverses to a lagging indicator (+3.32 steps) on OpenHands.\n75\t\n76\t\\paragraph{File Diff Frequency (file\\_diff).} Measures the rate of meaningful file modifications. The most objective signal, with 0.88 human agreement. Leads by $-$4.9 steps on OpenHands and $-$1.2 steps on SWE-Agent.\n77\t\n78\t\\paragraph{Outcome Backpropagation (OB).} Propagates task outcome labels backward through the trajectory, tagging late windows of failed tasks as stagnation. Lags by +6.7 steps (SWE-Agent) and +18.1 steps (OpenHands). Removing OB drops stagnation F1 by 22pp, confirming its unique signal contribution despite its privileged nature.\n79\t\n80\t\\paragraph{Aggregation.} Majority vote (MV) over the three LFs produces final labels, with a confidence score $\\text{conf} \\in \\{1, 2, 3\\}$ reflecting the number of agreeing LFs."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JPpAymd7RoVNiBpnEnu2yg","session_id":"7aba9a09-ed96-4e10-95c4-11fb49122c38","uuid":"aae32d48-7aa0-469c-86dd-e5394c82c0df","timestamp":"2026-05-14T22:24:01.100Z"}
